{{ .inputs.description }}

This is a protocol-2 plugin. Replace the **PLACEHOLDER** SDK dependency
`PROTOCOL_2_RELEASE_REQUIRED` with an SDK release explicitly supporting protocol 2.
SDK main now implements Serve v2, but a merged commit is not a published release:
keep the placeholder until a protocol-2 release exists. For local development,
render with `--input sdk_spec=file:/path/to/plugin-sdk.tgz`, built from SDK main.
The package's version alone is insufficient; verify its exported
`PROTOCOL_VERSION` is 2. No package is published by this scaffold. Node 22+ runs authoring tools; esbuild bundles the
worker for {{ .inputs.server_runtime }}. Deno is required for untrusted plugins;
Node workers are trusted-only. The host owns permissions and data/cache roots.

```sh
npm install               # creates package-lock.json; commit it
npm ci                    # subsequent reproducible installs
npm run typecheck
npm test                  # build, SDK harness, stdio, schema and reload tests
npm run verify
npm run dev
```

Edit `schema.json`: `tools` contain MCP `input_schema` JSON, while `config` and
`secrets` use manifest-v2 `config.fields` and `config.secrets` shapes verbatim.
Field defaults are strings, including boolean/numeric defaults. Select fields
carry `options`; secrets cannot carry defaults. Generation produces tool input
and config TypeScript types in `src/generated.ts`; tool dispatch validates inputs
against the same bounded schema subset. Supported tool schemas are closed objects,
arrays, strings, booleans, numbers, integers and primitive enums; unsupported
keywords fail generation. Implement handlers in `src/plugin.ts`, using the SDK
config reader for host-provided strings and resolved credentials. Add tool
handlers when adding declarations. No legacy ConfigFieldDef is generated.

`plugin.json` is the authoring declaration. Builds create a fresh private
`.plugin-build/stages/stage-*` directory, bundle directly to `bin/server.js`
{{ if .inputs.include_ui }}and `ui/index.js` (sandboxed-frame isolation **preference**, subject to host policy){{ end }},
then use `@hollis-labs/plugin-sdk/build` to inventory bytes/modes, compute the
specified tree digest, write canonical JSON `plugin.yaml`, and verify the tree.
The bundled `package.json` fixes ESM classification. No launcher, PATH lookup,
raw runtime flags or top-level `dist/` bundle layout is used. The latest verified
stage reference lives outside the payload. Never mutate an accepted stage; remove
old unused stages only after the host releases them. See the SDK
[authoring guide](https://github.com/hollis-labs/plugin-sdk/blob/main/docs/ts-plugin-authoring.md)
for the tree digest and review contract.

Dev mode polls source/schema changes, serially builds a fresh verified stage and
calls a developer-selected adapter. Copy `reload.example.json` to `reload.json`
and change its loopback HTTP URL, or use `{"command":["your-host-adapter","reload"]}`
(no shell; stage path appended as the final argument). HTTP sends
`{plugin_id,stage,tree_sha256}`. The adapter must adopt the same immutable tree,
review/verify it and ask the host to reload. This is a configurable seam, **not a
Nanite endpoint contract**. Failed builds/verification do not call reload.
Without an adapter dev mode only stages and verifies.

**Live protocol-2 reload remains held until plugin-host lifecycle and host adoption
land.** Tests use a stub HTTP receiver, not a production host. The SDK dependency
must support protocol-2 Serve for harness/stdio acceptance. SDK main supplies
required incarnation, capability-contract and grant fields through its harness;
the stdio test sends them explicitly. There is no protocol-1 or legacy-manifest
fallback. UI renderer modes are host-owned and remain separate.

To build a local SDK tarball from a sibling SDK checkout updated to main:

```sh
cd ../plugin-sdk/ts
npm ci
npm run build -w @hollis-labs/plugin-sdk
node --input-type=module -e 'import {PROTOCOL_VERSION} from "@hollis-labs/plugin-sdk"; if (PROTOCOL_VERSION !== 2) throw new Error("Protocol-2 SDK required")'
npm pack -w @hollis-labs/plugin-sdk
```

Pass that tarball through `sdk_spec` when rendering, or update the dependency to
a `file:` tarball spec and regenerate the lockfile with `npm install`. Commit
the lockfile; use `npm ci` for subsequent installs. This creates only a local
package, without publishing or tagging.
